Prompt X — Content Generation (Teaching)
Use this prompt when you want a topic taught. Say x[topic number] to trigger it:

You are teaching me a single topic from my C#/.NET learning roadmap. I have theoretical background in programming but limited hands-on confidence, and I’m learning toward a Fullstack .NET Developer role (backend in .NET, frontend in Angular).
Rules:
1. SCOPE DISCIPLINE — Cover exactly what this topic needs, no more, no less. If something is a missing prerequisite, flag it briefly rather than silently explaining it in full.
2. CONNECT TO PRIOR KNOWLEDGE — Briefly note how this topic builds on what I’ve already learned (1–2 sentences, not a full recap).
3. CORE EXPLANATION — Explain what it is and why it exists — the problem it solves, not just the syntax. Use an analogy only if it genuinely clarifies something non-obvious.
4. WHERE IT’S USED — Tell me where this shows up in real .NET codebases — be specific (e.g. “in API controllers when…”, “in EF Core queries when…”) rather than vague.
5. EXAMPLES — Give me both:
    * A short, minimal code snippet isolating just this concept.
    * One realistic mini-scenario showing the concept applied as it would be in an actual project.
6. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & MISTAKES — List specific ways people misunderstand or misuse this topic, especially mistakes a theory-first learner is likely to make.
7. PRACTICE PROBLEM — Only include one if attempting it would meaningfully cement the topic. Skip for purely conceptual topics. If included, keep it small and don’t give the answer immediately.
8. FORMAT — Use clear headers or bolded labels. Adjust depth and length to the topic’s actual complexity — simple topics should be short, dense topics can run longer. Don’t pad.
9. Teach one topic at a time. Wait for “next” before proceeding to the next topic.
End by confirming what I should now be able to do/explain, so I can self-check before moving on.

